  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a microbial electrochemical system in which electricity generated at an anode is coupled to useful chemical production at a cathode. On the left, water is oxidized at the anode to form oxygen, protons, and electrons. The electrons travel through an external circuit to the cathode, while protons cross a proton-exchange membrane. Rod-shaped microbes are shown attached to the cathode, indicating that microorganisms use these reducing equivalents to drive chemical conversions.\n\nThe right side highlights the range of cathodic reactions and products that can be obtained. These include oxygen reduction to either water or hydrogen peroxide, proton reduction to hydrogen, nitrate reduction to nitrogen gas, carbon dioxide reduction to acetate, and water reduction to hydrogen under alkaline conditions. A final example shows more complex bioproduction from glucose, yielding compounds such as butanol, glutamate, and propionate.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the main idea that electrochemical cells combined with microbial catalysis can channel electrons from simple oxidation reactions into a variety of value-added products, from small molecules like hydrogen and acetate to more complex fermentation chemicals.",
